{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"b1a8387a-9b0a-4deb-bb25-1a59a4f33145","step":{"id":"s1","script":"Start with a right triangle. Its perpendicular sides measure three and four; the side opposite the right angle is the hypotenuse.","ops":[{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"A1","content":"Squares on a right triangle"},{"op":"figure","id":"diagram","region":"right","frame":{"x":[-4.5,7.8],"y":[-3.8,7.8],"axes":"none"},"marks":[{"kind":"point","id":"vertex_o","at":[0,0]},{"kind":"point","id":"vertex_b","at":[3,0]},{"kind":"point","id":"vertex_c","at":[0,4]},{"kind":"polyline","id":"triangle","points":[{"at":"vertex_o"},{"at":"vertex_b"},{"at":"vertex_c"}],"closed":true},{"kind":"angle","at":{"at":"vertex_o"},"from":{"at":"vertex_b"},"to":{"at":"vertex_c"},"r":0.06},{"kind":"text","at":[1.5,0.35],"text":"3"},{"kind":"text","at":[0.35,2],"text":"4"},{"kind":"text","at":[1.8,2.3],"text":"c"}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"b1a8387a-9b0a-4deb-bb25-1a59a4f33145","step":{"id":"s2","script":"Build a square outward on each perpendicular side. Their areas are three squared, or nine, and four squared, or sixteen.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"leg_squares","on":"diagram","marks":[{"kind":"polyline","id":"square_three","points":[{"at":"vertex_o"},[0,-3],[3,-3],{"at":"vertex_b"}],"closed":true,"fill":true},{"kind":"polyline","id":"square_four","points":[{"at":"vertex_o"},{"at":"vertex_c"},[-4,4],[-4,0]],"closed":true,"fill":true},{"kind":"text","at":[1.5,-1.5],"text":"9"},{"kind":"text","at":[-2,2],"text":"16"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"leg_areas","anchor":{"el":"title","side":"below","gap":0.12},"latex":"3^2=9,\\qquad 4^2=16"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"b1a8387a-9b0a-4deb-bb25-1a59a4f33145","step":{"id":"s3","script":"The hypotenuse square has the combined area of the other two squares. Here, twenty-five square units means the hypotenuse measures five.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"hyp_square","on":"diagram","marks":[{"kind":"polyline","id":"square_c","points":[{"at":"vertex_b"},[7,3],[4,7],{"at":"vertex_c"}],"closed":true,"fill":true},{"kind":"text","at":[3.5,3.5],"text":"25"},{"kind":"text","at":[5.8,5.2],"text":"5"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"theorem","anchor":{"el":"leg_areas","side":"below","gap":0.12},"latex":"a^2+b^2=c^2"},{"op":"equation","id":"example","anchor":{"el":"theorem","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"c^2=9+16=25\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad c=5"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"For perpendicular sides of lengths 6 and 8, what is the area of the hypotenuse square?","expected_gist":"6 squared plus 8 squared equals 36 plus 64, or 100 square units."}}}
